The radius is the distance from the center \((3,-2)\) to the point \((0,2)\):
\[
r=\sqrt{(0-3)^2+(2+2)^2}
=\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2}
=\sqrt{9+16}
=\sqrt{25}
=5.
\]
Therefore the radius is \(5\).

Write the line as \(x-y+3=0\).
The distance from the center \(C(2,-1)\) to this line equals the radius:
\[
r=\frac{|2-(-1)+3|}{\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2}}
=\frac{|2+1+3|}{\sqrt{2}}
=\frac{6}{\sqrt{2}}
=3\sqrt{2}.
\]
Therefore the radius is \(3\sqrt{2}\).
